# Kubernetes and Helm safety gates

Before running any cluster or package-manager command against a cluster, classify it. A command that is not in the table is destructive until it is classified.

| Class | Gate |
| --- | --- |
| Read-only | Run it. Never print Secret data. |
| Local config | Allowed when the task needs it. Announce every context switch. |
| Remote write | Only when the user asked for this action and named the context and namespace. Preview first, summarize, then run. |
| Destructive or production | Show the exact command and the preview, then wait for an explicit yes to this command in this turn. |

Also:

- Writes name the context and namespace on the command. Do not trust the current context.
- If the context or namespace looks like production, or the user says it is production, treat it as destructive.
- Preview with a diff or a server dry-run before apply or upgrade.
- If a GitOps controller owns the resource, change the manifest repository instead of the cluster.
- Deny an image tag of `latest` or an untagged image. Use a commit id, a version, or a digest.
- An upgrade waits and rolls back itself on failure.
- After a write, report the command with secrets redacted, the context, the namespace, the rollout result, and the undo command.
- Do not delete with `--all`, all namespaces, or an unpreviewed label selector. Do not edit production resources in place. Do not pass secret literals on the command line.
